  - title: New Group Node
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 0
      y: 0
      width: 100
      height: 100
    contents: []
  stickyNoteInfos:
  - title: 'Capacity:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 702
      y: 85
      width: 243
      height: 302
    contents: "Capacity count is used for the memory allocation of a system. Increasing
      this number will increase the memory allocated.\r\n\r\nIt also serves as \u201Cmax
      active\u201D particles.\r\nIt's a good practice to keep this number as close
      as possible to the maximum number of alive particles.\r\n\r\nIn the VFX Control
      tab (click on the slider icon in the top-right corner of the VFX graph tabs),
      you can toggle the debug mode. This can help you set the capacity count.\r\n\r\nYou
      can try to decrease the capacity count to see how it affects the Max particle
      active.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 3
  - title: 'Set Size:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1418
      y: 621
      width: 419
      height: 249
    contents: "The \u201CSize\u201D attribute is responsible for the visual size
      of the particles on screen.\r\n\r\nA lot of \u201CSet Attribute\u201D blocks
      have a \u201CRandom\u201D option Built-in.\r\nJust click on it and change the
      random mode to uniform so that you can set the random range directly in the
      block.\r\n\r\nOur second \u201Cset size\u201D is overwriting the previous result.
      But instead of using the \u201Crandom\u201D option that you can find in the
      inspector, it uses a random operator node.\r\nThere's always several ways of
      doing the same thing in VFX Graph.\r\n\r\nThere is no right or wrong answer.
      It depends on how you like to work.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: 'Deactivate:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 377
      y: 673
      width: 377
      height: 152
    contents: "Those context blocks might be similar, but we're still paying for
      the operations. \r\nYou can deactivate one of the \u201Cset sizes\u201D by
      \u201Cunchecking\u201D the tick box in the upper-left corner of the block.\r\n\r\nAny
      context block can be activated or deactivated easily, and this can be used
      to compare different results quickly.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: 'Composition Mode:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1408
      y: 1376
      width: 498
      height: 234
    contents: "Most of the Block dealing with Attribute got a \"Composition\" mode
      present in the Inspector tab.\r\n\r\nThis allows specifying what kind of operation
      to perform on an Attribute.\r\nWe can Add, Set, Multiply or even blend to the
      existing Attribute.\r\n\r\nWe're using an \"Attribute from Curve\" Block. This
      allows us to modify the \"Size\" of the particles thanks to a curve. This curve
      can be sampled with the \"Life\", the \"Speed\" or even with custom particles'
      data.\r\nIn our case, we use the \"normalized\" Lifetime of the particles to
      sample the curves that allow us to Control the Particles \"Size\" with a curve
      across particle life.\r\n\r\nIn the inspector tab, the \"Composition Mode\"
      is set to Multiply, meaning that we're going to \"Multiply\" the Attributes'
      data that we set in the \u201CInit Context\u201D by this curve.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: 'Multiply Size :'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 601
      y: 1729
      width: 346
      height: 154
    contents: "This Block has been deactivated, as it's doing the same as the previous
      one. \r\nThe First one is using an \"Attribute from Curve\" Block, that modifies
      attributes based on the curve.\r\n\r\nThis shows how to do the same with a
      Regular \"Set Attribute\" block.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: 'Sampling Curves :'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 298
      y: 1504
      width: 250
      height: 193
    contents: 'Curves and Gradient are really helpful when it comes to "visually"
      controlling /modifying data, but they need 0-1 value to sample them.



      The
      "Age over Lifetime" block is the normalized 0-1 lifetime of the particles.

      This
      allows us to Read/Sample curves and gradients with our Lifetime representing
      the X value.

'
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: 
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1436
      y: 89
      width: 242
      height: 108
    contents: You can try to decrease the capacity count to see how it impacts the
      max particle active.
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: Turbulance
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1453
      y: 1001
      width: 268
      height: 100
    contents: "\u566A\u58F0\u6270\u52A8\n"
    theme: Classic
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 1
  categories: []
  uiBounds:
    serializedVersion: 2
    x: 0
    y: -359
    width: 1931
    height: 2527
